  "proof": {
    "preservationUpperBound": "For any preserved set S, all other tree degrees>=1 implies sum_{i in S}(d_i−1)<=N−2. The smallest r+1 costs have minimum sum among all r+1 sets, so the affordable prefix gives a cardinality upper bound.",
    "remainder": "If j is the first rejected vertex, remaining b<d_j−1; target_j=1+b<d_j. All later vertices have cost>0 and stay1, so none is accidentally preserved.",
    "allSelected": "Source connectivity, or the degree API sum contract, gives sumd>=2N−2. If every cost fits, sumd<=2N−2 too; equality implies remainder0.",
    "realization": "A positive target sequence with sum2N−2 makes a Pruefer word repeating i exactlytarget_i−1 times. Remaining degree equals remaining multiplicity+1; each chosen leaf is distinct from the current word letter. Deletion/attachment ensures connected simple tree and exact degrees.",
    "generalizationLimit": "Different preservation rewards or original-edge constraints change the optimization; this API does not apply the cheap-cost rule to those variants.",
    "unconfirmedLemmas": []
  },
